WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:10.530
Have you ever wondered what happens when someone in England visits the world Science festivals webpage?

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:19.319
First their computer needs to ask the world science festival server for a copy of that webpage

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:27.539
The computer sticks this request into a virtual envelope called a packet,  wrapped with specific information about that request

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:31.019
including the world Science Festival's IP address

00:00:32.079 --> 00:00:37.529
The computer sends this packet out of the house and below the street via large underground copper wires

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:43.680
It passes through small regional networks before ending up here at Tellehouse North in London

00:00:43.989 --> 00:00:51.899
Tella House North is England's main internet hub the IP address on this packet tells the hub that the world science festival server is

00:00:52.059 --> 00:00:53.739
actually in Los Angeles.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:01:00.900
So Tella house north sends the packet out as light across the atlantic, over fibre optic cables very deep beneath the ocean

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:08.300
The packet ends up here 60 Hudson street, New York City the largest internet hub on the east coast

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:16.660
This Hub sends the packet through a series of regional networks connecting New York to Los Angeles where the world science festival server resides?

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:21.839
The server reads the requests and gets ready to send the webpage to England

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:28.109
But web pages made up of images and text are too large to send us a single packet of data

00:01:28.509 --> 00:01:30.839
So how do we get it back to England?

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:37.559
imagine a group of 5,000 tourists visiting New York City in a single gigantic tour bus

00:01:38.049 --> 00:01:42.539
They are way up in Harlem, but they want to visit the Statue of Liberty before it closes

00:01:42.939 --> 00:01:45.179
But it's rush hour on a Friday

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:50.519
There's no way that giant bus is going to fit through those crazy congested streets

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.339
So they decide to get off the bus and spread out

00:01:53.890 --> 00:02:01.259
Some take the Subway, some take cabs, a few rent bikes, and some even take Kayaks down the Hudson River

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:05.219
How they get there doesn't matter as long as they get there on time

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:13.660
Likewise for the internet to work efficiently this webpage is pulverized into thousands of tiny packets of data

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:18.819
Each one wrapped with all of the information it needs to rebuild itself in England

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:26.140
The packets are sent to LA's One Wilshire hub which checks the traffic report before sending them off

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:31.000
through miles and miles of [lam] they travel checking in through different hubs like

00:02:31.519 --> 00:02:37.779
our New York city tourists, those packets don't care how they get there as long as they get there as fast as possible

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.530
Most of them will go through 60 Hudson in New York where they are redirected back to England as light?

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:47.289
Riding a fibre of glass as thick as a silver dollar

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:54.370
then back on Copper wire through regional British networks until all the package reach their destination and

00:02:55.129 --> 00:02:56.870
and

00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:01.149
This Epic Journey it all happens in about a second

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:09.549
Along with trillions upon trillions of similar Journeys that happen each and every day on this remarkable easy to take for granted

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:13.149
Network of Networks we call the internet

